---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-sendlane';
const title = "Drip vs Sendlane for Shopify Email: 2026 Comparison";
const desc = "Compare Drip workflow entry and history with Sendlane continuity, Privy service scope and queue handover.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does the Privy acquisition mean Sendlane is closed?",
    "answer": "The acquisition announcement establishes ownership context, not closure of a particular account. Verify your current product, contract, integration and support path with the supplier."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should decide Drip versus a Sendlane/Privy proposal?",
    "answer": "Compare the same entry, re-entry, delayed-send and history fixtures. Drip is a candidate for operator-maintained event workflows; contracted support can be useful when its deliverables match the work your team needs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will importing history restart workflows?",
    "answer": "Do not assume so. Drip documents forward-facing triggers. Distinguish imported attributes from events, decide how pending journeys continue and test replay and duplicate handling in the actual destination."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should migration and pricing be evaluated?",
    "answer": "Use current quotes and an explicit inventory of records, permission, history and pending messages. Assign old and new queue owners, acceptance and rollback. The 15,000- and 18,000-contact fixtures are quote inputs, not prices or migration schedules."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Sendlane for Shopify Email: 2026 Comparison</h1>
<p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">The useful decision is who will own lifecycle logic after setup: your operator, a contracted service team, or both. Evaluate Drip’s event-driven workflows against the actual Sendlane or Privy account and service being offered, with special attention to history, re-entry and pending messages during a handover.</p>
<p class="mt-5 text-cyan-100/90">The scenarios are proposed evaluations, not observed merchant results. Product scope and service commitments need confirmation for the account being purchased.</p></header>
<div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl"><TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Operator-owned logic", "tool": "Drip", "reason": "Evaluate workflow triggers, event dependencies and safe ongoing edits."}, {"label": "Service-led continuity", "tool": "Sendlane / Privy", "reason": "Verify the current account, contracted help and acceptance responsibilities."}, {"label": "Migration decision", "tool": "Inventory first", "reason": "Preserve history and resolve old queues before enabling replacements."}]} /></div>
<div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto"><h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">What to compare</h2><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Drip</th><th>Sendlane / Privy proposal</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Entry behavior</td><td>Triggers apply to future actions; a person cannot enter while already in that workflow</td><td>Demonstrate entry and re-entry behavior in the proposed account</td></tr>
<tr><td>History</td><td>Separate imported fields from triggering events</td><td>Confirm what history transfers and what needs reconstruction</td></tr>
<tr><td>Support</td><td>Name the merchant operator and any contracted help</td><td>Name the migration, review and ongoing support deliverables</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Quote the required contacts, sends and work</td><td>Quote the actual product and service scope, not a historical package</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Sendlane continuity is part of the evaluation</h2>
<p>Privy’s <a href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">February 2, 2026 acquisition announcement</a> identifies Sendlane as an email and SMS platform with a service-first approach. Its <a href="https://www.privy.com/sendlane-acquisition">acquisition page</a> discusses email, SMS and onsite tools. This establishes a changed supplier context; it does not establish that a particular Sendlane account has closed or that every account has identical migration terms.</p>
<p>For an existing Sendlane customer, ask which product, login, integration, contract and support team continue for that account. For a new proposal, ask which environment you would actually buy. Obtain written scope for data transfer, flow rebuilding, deliverability review, authentication and ongoing edits. Service can be a positive fit for a lean ecommerce team when deliverables and merchant responsibilities are explicit.</p>
<h2>Drip: entry rules matter more than a canvas label</h2>
<p>Drip’s <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424702655501-Workflows-Triggers">workflow trigger guide</a> describes event-based entry, forward-facing triggers and the restriction that a person cannot enter a workflow while already in it. That makes Drip a useful candidate for an operator who wants to maintain event-driven journeys. It does not prove that importing old orders automatically reenacts their workflows.</p>
<p>Inventory the provider, event name, stable identifier, timestamp and fields required by each entry rule. Test one customer triggering again while waiting, then triggering after exit. Distinguish intended repeat entry from duplicate retries. Record the behavior in both candidate accounts rather than assuming they share the same semantics.</p>
<h2>Home goods rebuild: preserve history without replaying everything</h2>
<p>For a hypothetical home goods store leaving neglected Klaviyo journeys, inventory welcome, cart, post-purchase and winback before discussing implementation speed. Separate current profile attributes, historical orders, consent evidence, suppressions and messages already sent. A contact import does not preserve every event dependency or delay position.</p>
<p>Choose explicitly whether a customer waiting in an old sequence finishes there, stops entirely or enters a specific replacement step. Keep a handover ledger with customer, journey, version, last completed step, pending job, sender and cancellation status. Verify old queue cancellation before the new owner enables the replacement; turning off entry may not cancel already scheduled work.</p>
<p>Compare self-managed rebuilding with the contracted Sendlane/Privy service against this same inventory. Ask who resolves an unmapped field, who approves templates and who handles rollback. There is no documented two-week rebuild, avoided agency bill or positive ROI from this scenario.</p>
<h2>Pet supplies: bag size, replenishment and re-entry</h2>
<p>Use a pet supplies customer with purchases in two bag sizes. Build replenishment by the actual item, quantity and relevant usage assumptions; do not treat a fixed interval as established consumption. Check how a new order changes eligibility while a reminder waits. A canceled subscription and a skipped delivery need separate treatment if the subscription app supplies those states.</p>
<p>Test source-specific welcome from popup and footer, a first-category preference and wholesale exclusions. Identify which values are current fields and which are historical events. Change a preference while the customer waits and record which value the next branch reads. An operator-owned Drip program can fit a team that wants to inspect and maintain these rules; supported implementation can fit a team that needs help maintaining them.</p>
<p>Before a promotion, change one branch, inspect customers already in delays and roll back the version. Replay an order and retry a failed integration job. The acceptance result should show the intended replenishment entry once and no stale acquisition or winback discount after purchase. These are proposed tests, not supplier outcomes.</p>
<h2>Audio welcome and cart: a long consideration window</h2>
<p>For a hypothetical 140-day audio buying cycle, separate warranty or service information from marketing education and cross-sell. Establish purpose and permission for each message. Avoid labeling a customer dormant solely because a short default winback interval elapsed.</p>
<p>Use illustrative carts from $110 to $175 to test multi-item rendering, variant images, stock changes and an optional 10% incentive against education. On a $110 eligible subtotal the incentive is $11; on $175 it is $17.50 before fees. Define subtotal basis, currency and stacking rather than claiming either platform recovered these carts.</p>
<p>Keep VIP treatment separate from discount-seeking behavior and include recent purchasers and active service issues in review. At dispatch, verify current eligibility, opt-out, purchase and subscription state wherever the chosen design requires it. Trace late cart updates arriving after purchase. Include Shopify’s recovery sender and every external sender in the queue inventory; no supplier automatically guarantees suppression across all systems.</p>
<h2>Service handover: acceptance has a named owner</h2>
<p>Ask the proposed service team to demonstrate one completed flow, one failed event and one cancellation. Contracted help should specify whether it includes field mapping, creative revisions, suppression transfer, sender authentication, deliverability consultation and post-launch changes. Do not infer quarterly reviews or complete flow recreation from a general promise of support.</p>
<p>Give the merchant pause authority and keep an escalation contact for integration failures. Record event time, ingestion time, queue time and dispatch time. Test missing timezone, changed phone number, unsubscribe while waiting, STOP/HELP and reply routing for supported SMS markets. A successful export does not establish that withdrawal reached the destination sender.</p>
<p>Read live popup, checkout and keyword disclosures for the actual market and channels. Preserve source, wording, timestamp and withdrawal. Configure applicable quiet hours and frequency limits, audit disclosure changes against active journeys, and assign the reply inbox. This operational review does not establish legal or delivery clearance.</p>
<h2>Quote ongoing work, not an assumed support premium</h2>
<p>Retain 15,000 and 18,000 contacts as quote fixtures. Specify billable contacts, suppressed records, email sends, SMS destinations, onboarding and ongoing review. Compare current written terms, migration labor and operator time over the same period. Drip is not automatically cheaper, and Sendlane support is not automatically a premium or a substitute for merchant approval.</p>
<p>For a proposed campaign, contribution per incremental order must cover platform allocation, message and carrier costs, discounts and operational work. Track opt-outs by flow and full-price repeat purchases. Use randomized eligible holdouts and uncertainty reporting when estimating incremental recovery rather than treating attributed revenue as causal lift.</p>
<p>Choose Drip when the demonstrated workflow behavior and maintenance ownership suit your team. Retain or choose the offered Sendlane/Privy environment when continuity and contracted service meet the same acceptance tests. An alternative such as Sequenzy can be assessed against the inventory, but changing platforms is not a cure for unowned events, queues or approvals.</p>
</article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip">Klaviyo vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip">Sequenzy vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/alternatives/klaviyo-alternative">Best Klaviyo alternatives</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/high-aov">Email apps for high-AOV stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/alternatives/drip-alternative">Drip alternatives</a>
        </p>
      </div>

</div></section>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Sendlane FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
